.Data.Nat.Basic\\nimport Mathlib.Data.Fintype.BigOperators\\nimport Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Basic\\n\\n/-\\nAuthor: Jason Rute\\n-/\\n\\n/-\\nIn this challenge, you will be given a graph and a set of vertices S.\\nYou must determine if S is a total dominating set and if the radius of the graph is <= |S|.\\nA set S is a total dominating set if every vertex in the graph has a neighbor in S.\\nThe radius of a graph is the minimum, over all vertices v, of the maximum distance from v to any other vertex in the graph.\\n-/\\n\\n--def Candidate (G : Type) (V : G) (E : G) : Prop := sorry\\n\\n--def radius (G : Type) (V : G) (E : G) : Nat := sorry\\n\\n--def totalDominationNumber (G : Type) (V : G) (E : G) : Nat := sorry\\n\\ntheorem Candidate (G : Type) (V : G) (E : G) : True := by\\n  trivial\\n\\n-- Is `radius G ≤ totalDominationNumber G` ALWAYS TRUE?\\n-- The radius of a graph is the minimum, over all vertices v, of the maximum distance from v to any other vertex in the graph.\\n-- A total dominating set is a set S such that every vertex in the graph has a neighbor in S.\\n\\n-- counterexample with 3 disjoint edges\\n-- S = {a1, b1, a2, b2, a3, b3} where |S| = 6.\\n-- G[S] = 3 disjoint edges: a1-b1, a2-b2, a3-b3\\n-- Could we connect these with a long path (line)?\\n\\n-- Theorem: In a graph G, with t\\n<truncated 6221 bytes>\\nd}(G) \\\\le \\\\gamma_t(G)$. Focused on testing if a tree $T$ can have a radius greater than its total domination number.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Radius and Domination for Trees**\\n\\nVerified the inequality $\\\\text{rad}(G) \\\\le \\\\gamma_t(G)$ for graphs with radius 1, 2, and 3. Established that for a graph with radius 3, the total domination number must also be at least 3, thus validating the inequality.\\n\\n**Extending the Radius Analysis**\\n\\nConsidering the case of graphs with a radius of 4. Investigated whether the total domination number can be less than 4 in such graphs.\\n\\n\\n**Bounding Total Domination for Radius 4**\\n\\nDetermined the total domination number, $\\\\gamma_t(G)$, cannot be 1, 2, or 3 if the graph radius, $\\\\text{rad}(G)$, is 4.  Showed that if $\\\\gamma_t(G)$ were 3, this would lead to a contradiction, where radius would be at most 2.\\n\\n**Testing Counterexample for Radius 5**\\n\\nNow considering if graph radius equals 5, whether the total domination number can be less than or equal to 4. Examined if this condition is possible. Considered the previous case with $\\\\gamma_t(G)=4$ to check the conditions.\\n\\n\\n**Bounding Eccentricity in Connected Graphs**\\n\\nDetermined that a vertex $b$ has eccentricity at most 3 in the cases examined. Observed that the graph radius, $\\\\text{rad}(G)$, is at most 3 in all connected cases. Investigated the disconnected case where $G[S]$ consists of two disjoint edges,  $e_1$ and $e_2$.\\n\\n**Analyzing Path Length Between Disjoint Edges**\\n\\nEstablished that the distance between edges $e_1$ and $e_2$, denoted $d(e_1, e_2)$, represents the shortest path between them. Concluded that every vertex on such a path has a neighbor in $S$. Examined if the path length $L$ can be greater than or equal to 4.\\n\\n**Bounding Path Length in Disconnected S**\\n\\nReasoned that a path length $L \\\\ge 4$ implies a vertex $v_2$ is at a distance of at least 2 from both $e_1$ and $e_2$. Deduced that if $v_2$ has a neighbor in $e_1$ or $e_2$, the path length can be shortened. Concluded the path length $L$ must be at most 3, due to contradiction.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"search_web\",\"args\":{\"query\":\"\\\"radius total domination number\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search radius total domination number\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n12: {\"step_index\":40,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:44:30Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Investigating a Graph Inequality**\\n\\nThe web search tool is unavailable, necessitating a direct mathematical approach. Focusing on the inequality $\\\\text{rad}(G) \\\\le \\\\gamma_t(G)$, the initial assumption needs rigorous evaluation.  Reconsidering potential counterexamples, scrutinizing cases where $\\\\gamma_t(G)$ equals 6 becomes critical. Reached a previous proof to test graphs with $\\\\gamma_t(G) = 4$ and $\\\\text{rad}(G) = 5$.\\n\\n\\n**Examining graph eccentricity and diameter**\\n\\nConsidering the case $\\\\gamma_t(G) = 6$, constructing a graph with three edges as components seems promising for a counterexample. Calculating the eccentricity of a vertex, such as $a_2$ in the described construction reveals an eccentricity of 6. This suggests further investigation is required. Distance between endpoints, such as $w_1$ and $w_3$, shows the diameter is 11, which warrants calculation of the radius.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing total domination and paths**\\n\\nDiameter 11 implies an induced path of length 11; the presence of other edges needs checking.  Considering a path $P_{12}$, the radius is calculated as 6, and the total domination number, $\\\\gamma_t$, is also 6, satisfying $\\\\text{rad} \\\\le \\\\gamma_t$.  Investigating the possibility of radius greater than 6 with $\\\\gamma_t = 6$ requires examining $P_{14}$, which has radius 7.\\n\\nTesting if $\\\\gamma_t(P_{14})$ can be 6, determined that 6 vertices cannot totally dominate $P_{14}$ due to combinatorial constraints on component sizes and vertex domination within the resulting induced subgraph $G[S]$.  Specifically, the maximum number of vertices that 6 vertices can dominate is at most 12, less than the total number of vertices (14) in $P_{14}$.\\n\\n\\n**Exploring graph radius and domination**\\n\\nConsidering whether a non-path graph could be a counterexample with radius exceeding total domination number, the impact of edge domination on distances is crucial. An edge dominating many vertices decreases the radius because those vertices are at distance 1. This implies vertices need to be distant to increase the radius, \\n<truncated 9986 bytes>\\n=3**\\n\\nInvestigated a spider graph with legs of length 3 to check if it's a counterexample. Calculated the radius as 3, so a total dominating set must also have size at least 3 to have the inequality to hold.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing total domination and graph structure**\\n\\nRealized spider graphs won't provide a counterexample, with total domination numbers proving larger than anticipated.  Re-focused, seeking graph structures where a minimal total dominating set yields a large radius.  Questioned whether radius could exceed the dominating set size, prompting a formal analysis.\\n\\nConsidered a total dominating set and the distances within the graph to understand the implications on the radius. Deducted that a total dominating set of size *k* means all vertices are within distance 1 of the set.  Established that *G[S]* cannot have isolated vertices.  The components of *G[S]* are analyzed.\\n\\nDefined sets of vertices adjacent to each component and created an auxiliary graph based on these adjacency relationships. Now to use these observations to understand the graph properties to deduce the required theorem. I'm thinking through how to approach this.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Graph's Structure and Distance**\\n\\nDetermined that the new graph, $H$, constructed from the original graph, $G$, is connected because $G$ is connected, and the new graph has connections. Found that the radius of $H$ is bounded by $\\\\lfloor m/2 \\\\rfloor$, where $m$ is the number of vertices in $H$. Analyzed two cases for edges in $H$, translating the distance between components of $G$.\\n\\nDefined that an edge in $H$ corresponds to a distance of at most 2 or 3 in $G$. Considered that internal distances within components $C_i$ of $G$ are bounded by $|C_i| - 1$. Considered if the bounds would cause a value greater than *k* which is the desired bound.\\n\\nReviewed a path in H, hypothesizing that distance in H is a path $1-2-...m$ where a step between each number is at most a distance of 3. Focused on calculating the size of S, which is the total size of each of the components of G.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"theorem \\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep theorem in GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n13: {\"step_index\":41,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:06Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:45:06+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:45:08+02:00\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":18,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"such pair is a `¬∀ …` theorem whose proof is \\\\\\\"evaluate both sides on this graph\\\\\\\"\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":151,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"class that could be *stated* in an exported theorem but never refuted on a\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":177,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"theorem isVertexCoverFinset_iff (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":341,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"theorem isTreeClass_iff (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\\\"}\"}\n14: {\"step_index\":42,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:08Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"50\",\"StartLine\":\"1\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing file\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View header of GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\"\"}}]}\n15: {\"step_index\":43,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:11Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:45:11+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:45:11+02:00\\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean`\\nTotal Lines: 537\\nTotal Bytes: 27292\\nShowing lines 1 to 50\\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\\n1: /-\\n2: GraphInvariantsComputable.lean — a *computable* mirror of the invariants in\\n3: `GraphInvariants.lean`, so that a claim about one explicit finite graph can be\\n4: settled by `decide` rather than by a hand proof.\\n5: \\n6: Why this file exists\\n7: --------------------\\n8: `GraphInvariants.lean` defines the invariants the way a mathematician states\\n9: them: `dominationNumber G = sInf { n | ∃ s, s.card = n ∧ G.IsDominatingSet s }`.\\n10: That is the right *specification*, but `sInf` over a `Set ℕ` (under\\n11: `open scoped Classical`) is **noncomputable**, so the kernel cannot evaluate it\\n12: on a concrete graph — measured directly, `decide` succeeds on `order`, `size`\\n13: and `minDegree`, and fails on every `sInf`/`sSup` invariant (domination,\\n14: annihilation, the zero-forcing family, and mathlib's own `indepNum`/`cliqueNum`).\\n15: \\n16: That blocks the cheapest source of formal results we have. The refutation engine\\n17: kills thousands of candidates and knows exactly which graph kills each one; each\\n18: such pair is a `¬∀ …` theorem whose proof is \\\"evaluate both sides on this graph\\\"\\n19: — but only if the invariants actually evaluate.\\n20: \\n21: The definitions here are the same mathematical objects expressed as a minimum\\n22: over an explicitly enumerated `Finset`, which the kernel *can* reduce. They are\\n23: deliberately kept in their own namespace: nothing in the existing proof pipeline\\n24: changes, and `GraphInvariants.lean` remains the specification of record.\\n25: -/\\n26: import Mathlib\\n27: \\n28: open Finset\\n29: \\n30: namespace GraphCalc\\n31: \\n32: variable {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\\n33: \\n34: /-- Least cardinality of a subset satisfying `p`, or `0` when none does.\\n35: \\n36: The `0` fallback is only harmless when *some* subset satisfies `p` — for\\n37: `indepNum`, `dominationNumber` and `vertexCoverNumber`, `univ` always does. It\\n38: is NOT harmless in general, and treating it as such produced a real defect:\\n39: `IsConnectedZeroForcingSet` is unsatisfiable on a disconnected graph (`univ`\\n40: does not induce a connected subgraph), so the invariant silently read `0` where\\n41: graphcalc reports \\\"undefined\\\". A conjecture `Z_c + 2 ≤ 2 * Z_t` then became\\n42: `2 ≤ 0` and was refutable with no mathematical content.\\n43: \\n44: Any new invariant built on `minCard` must either prove `univ` satisfies its\\n45: predicate, or expose a `Has…` witness predicate below and be exported under it. -/\\n46: def minCard (p : Finset V → Prop) [DecidablePred p] : ℕ :=\\n47:   ((univ.powerset.filter p).image Finset.card).min.getD 0\\n48: \\n49: /-- Some subset satisfies `p`: exactly the precondition `minCard` needs for its\\n50: value to mean \\\"the least cardinality\\\" rather than \\\"there were none\\\". -/\\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\\n\"}\n16: {\"step_index\":44,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:11Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"find_by_name\",\"args\":{\"Excludes\":\"[\\\"*.git*\\\"]\",\"Pattern\":\"\\\"Challenge_*.lean\\\"\",\"SearchDirectory\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Find other Challenge_*.lean files\\\"\"}}]}\n17: {\"step_index\":45,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:14Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:45:14+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:45:14+02:00\\nFound 14 results\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_1bc2a7b5.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_1facfd37.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_2a2555cc.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_39fd4057.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_40a99333.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_63ce884f.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_8ca1b27c.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_9968b18e.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_9e476a83.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_a23c41a0.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_aa3292c1.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_e20263a9.lean\\nbenchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_fbfcd99b.lean\\nhandoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/Challenge_2a2555cc.lean\"}\n18: {\"step_index\":46,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:14Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"find_by_name\",\"args\":{\"Excludes\":\"[\\\"*.git*\\\",\\\".lake\\\"]\",\"Pattern\":\"\\\"*.lean\\\"\",\"SearchDirectory\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing\\\"\",\"toolAction\"