:   \\\"statement\\\": \\\"(nontrivial) \\\\u21d2 sub_total_domination_number \\\\u2264 (2 \\\\u00b7 slater)\\\",\\n116:   \\\"label\\\": \\\"open\\\",\\n117:   \\\"label_source\\\": \\\"audit_top100\\\",\\n118:   \\\"stratum\\\": \\\"promising:universal\\\",\\n119:   \\\"witness_g6\\\": null,\\n120:   \\\"challenge_sha256\\\": \\\"0ff3644fef2c5d9d78df5fe327c038899e23ba2d8290d8ec38389b32d45001b2\\\"\\n121:  },\\n122:  {\\n123:   \\\"id\\\": \\\"1bc2a7b5\\\",\\n124:   \\\"file\\\": \\\"Challenge_1bc2a7b5.lean\\\",\\n125:   \\\"statement\\\": \\\"(nontrivial) \\\\u21d2 ((1/2) \\\\u00b7 sub_total_domination_number) \\\\u2264 slater\\\",\\n126:   \\\"label\\\": \\\"open\\\",\\n127:   \\\"label_source\\\": \\\"audit_top100\\\",\\n128:   \\\"stratum\\\": \\\"promising:universal\\\",\\n129:   \\\"witness_g6\\\": null,\\n130:   \\\"challenge_sha256\\\": \\\"2aec1eced58ebd1a0db562a82063167170ccea84dc7dc03418675506cd856b10\\\"\\n131:  }\\n132: ]\\nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\\n\",\"truncated_fields\":[\"content\"]}\n16: {\"step_index\":58,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:29Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Reviewing Conjectures and Search Scope**\\n\\nObserved that all 13 conjectures are labeled as \\\"open.\\\" These conjectures withstood small-graph counterexample searches. The search likely spanned up to graphs of 8, 9, or 10 vertices. Needs verification of the graph search size to confirm.\\n\\n\\n**Investigating Graphs and Relationships**\\n\\nWondering if a graph can exist where the radius exceeds the total domination number. Hypothesizing a scenario where $\\\\gamma_t = 6$ and $\\\\text{rad} \\\\ge 7$ to test this possibility. The diameter would also need to be at least 7 given the radius.\\n\\nConsidering a total dominating set of size 6, every vertex must be within distance 1. If the graph's radius is 7, can this total domination occur? Need to re-examine the distances between the components of the total dominating set.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Component Distances**\\n\\nInvestigated the distance between components. Adjacent components can be at a distance of at most 3 or 2. Determined that the number of components $m$ is at most $\\\\gamma_t(G)/2$.\\n\\nInferred that the component graph has a radius of at most $\\\\lfloor m/2 \\\\rfloor$. Therefore, every component $C_i$ is within this radius from the central component $C^*$.\\n\\n\\n**Calculating Distance to a Vertex**\\n\\nConsidering the distance in the graph $G$ from the central component $C^*$ to any vertex $x$.  The component graph distance between $C^*$ and any component $C_i$ containing $x$ is at most $\\\\lfloor m/2 \\\\rfloor$.  Each \\\"step\\\" in the component graph corresponds to a maximum distance of 2 edges in $G$ and an edge of distance 1 within the component.\\n\\nThe path through the components $C^* = D_0, D_1, \\\\dots, D_p = C_i$ leads to a total distance of at most $3p - 1$ within $G$, where $p \\\\le \\\\lfloor m/2 \\\\rfloor$.  Therefore, the distance in $G$ from $C^*$ to $x$ is at most $3 \\\\lfloor m/2 \\\\rfloor - 1$.\\n\\n\\n**Bounding Graph Radius with Total Domination**\\n\\nCalculated the distance from a central component to any vertex, concluding $\\\\text{dist}(u_0, x) \\\\le 3 \\\\lfloor m/2 \\\\rfloor + 1$.  Confirmed that $3 \\\\lfloor m/2 \\\\rfloor + 1 \\\\le 2m$ holds for all $m \\\\ge 1$, which is equivalent to $\\\\text{rad}(G) \\\\le \\\\gamma_t(G)$.\\n\\nConsidering components of size $\\\\ge 3$ further reduces the upper bound on the radius.  Deducted that $\\\\text{rad}(G) \\\\le \\\\gamma_t(G)$ is a general theorem for connected graphs.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"ballWithin\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep ballWithin\\\"\"}}]}\n17: {\"step_index\":59,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:40Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:45:40+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:45:40+02:00\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":503,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"def ballWithin (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ → V → Finset V\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":506,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"      let B := ballWithin G k v\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":511,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"  minUpTo (Fintype.card V) (fun k =\\\\u003e ballWithin G k v = univ)\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":519,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"  ∀ v : V, ballWithin G (Fintype.card V) v = univ\\\"}\"}\n18: {\"step_index\":60,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:45:40Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"radius\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep radius in lean_project\\\"\"}}]}\n19: {\"step_index\":62,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:02Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"radius\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/LeanProject/GraphInvariants.lean\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep radius in GraphInvariants.lean\\\"\"}}]}\n20: {\"step_index\":63,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:05Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:46:05+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:46:06+02:00\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/LeanProject/GraphInvariants.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":164,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"-- `radius` is mathlib's (`SimpleGraph.radius : ℕ∞`, the minimum eccentricity);\\\"}\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/LeanProject/GraphInvariants.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":165,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"-- the exporter reads it as `(G.radius).toNat`. On a disconnected graph it is\\\"}\"}\n21: {\"step_index\":64,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:06Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Defining the Conjecture**\\n\\nThe goal is to formally define the candidate conjecture that is under test. The `Candidate` proposition is defined using a universal quantifier over a type `V` with `Fintype` and `DecidableEq` instances, a simple graph `G` on `V` with a `DecidableRel` instance, which defines the conjecture. It seems that the conjecture is related to graph invariants like the radius and the total domination number.\\n\\n\\n**Re-evaluating the Conjecture**\\n\\nThe imports indicate a computational approach, but `GraphInvariants` is missing. The previous refutation might be flawed, possibly due to an incorrect assumption. A new attempt to construct a counterexample is needed, exploring graphs beyond simple classes like paths, trees, and cycles. Critically, there's doubt about the generality of the prior calculations and if the conjecture holds universally for all graphs.\\n\\n\\n**Verifying Path Distance**\\n\\nA detailed proof shows that if a graph's induced subgraph on a set `S` has two components, the distance between those components in the original graph is at most 3, not 4. The argument hinges on the fact that vertices outside `S` must have neighbors within `S`, leading to shorter connecting paths.\\n\\nExtending this to three components, a path of length 4 between two components is possible. This is because a vertex can connect to a third component, creating a distance of 2 from either of the original two. However, the exact configuration of a three-component graph needs further scrutiny.\\n\\n\\n**Testing Graph Extensions**\\n\\nInvestigating if adding a vertex to the graph increases the total domination set's size.  Adding vertex 13 and connecting it only to 12 requires adding 12 to the set, making |S| = 7. Connecting vertex 13 to 10 or 11 does not affect the radius, because the maximum distance from vertex 6 to vertex 13 would be 5, which is still less than or equal to the radius. Adding a vertex at both ends similarly requires adding vertices to the set to maintain total domination.\\n\\n\\n**Proving an Important Inequality**\\n\\nAnalyzing path distances and connections, it appears constructing a graph to violate $\\\\text{rad}(G) \\\\le \\\\gamma_t(G)$ is impossible. The argument relies on shortest path domination by the total dominating set. With `k` vertices in the total dominating set, the maximum path length dominated is limited.\\n\\nThe longest possible shortest path is bounded by $2k$ due to how the dominating set can cover the path. The radius, in turn, is at most $k$, confirming that the radius is less than or equal to the total domination number for any connected graph. Thus, a proof for `prove_2a2555cc` is achievable.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"def order\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep def order\\\"\"}}]}\n22: {\"step_index\":65,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:15Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:46:15+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:46:15+02:00\\n{\\\"File\\\":\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\",\\\"LineNumber\\\":67,\\\"LineContent\\\":\\\"def order (_G : SimpleGraph V) : ℕ := Fintype.card V\\\"}\"}\n23: {\"step_index\":66,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:15Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"false\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"def minUpTo\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep def minUpTo\\\"\"}}]}\n24: {\"step_index\":67,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:46:17Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:46:17+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2